			<div class="content" id="post-content-4851503">
									

<p><strong>Seeking Greenlights:</strong> Yes</p>
<p><strong>Page Type:</strong> SCP Article</p>
<p><strong>Elevator Pitch:</strong> This SCP is a phenomenon in which nobody can read any information documented about it. You cannot speak of it, cannot read it, cannot hear of it, and cannot see it. Containment procedures (Hints to the entity hidden behind the phenomenon) include an average sized concrete room sealed with two gates and guarded by 3 guards at all times. When entering it's chamber, no less than 4 guards should enter accompanying any other personelle. Cameras are prohibited as they do not work. The actual entity itself (though undescribable) is a large, completely black entity covered in a fur-like substance, which consists of 62% Steel. The entity is very aggressive, and shows the ability to remove information from documents of any kind stored on nearby devices and papers. It is very tough, and can ensure much punishment. People who make eye contact with the entity feel very strong symptoms if anxiety and stress, which wears off a few minutes after.</p>
<p><strong>Central Narrative:</strong> This SCP came into existence when a scientist attempted to document an anomaly that had been discovered by the foundation, only to discover that he could not type the words, he had a very powerful urge not to.</p>
<p><strong>Hook/Attention-Grabber:</strong> This SCP is focused around the idea of 'Wait, it's all Redacted? Well then what is it?' Readers can assume their wildest thoughts after reading containment procedures, which allows the reader some info, put not enough to know exactly what it is. Personelle have come up with a general code when discussing the entity, as experiments showed that it can be referred to by a name. Most people of the Site it's contained at know what it is, (As you can actually see an interact with the anomaly, bit you can't describe it or document it) but the reader doesn't. At the bottom of the article, there could be interviews with people trying to talk about it. I have came up with the idea that there could be a loophole in the phenomenon in which it can be described using something such as morse code or anything similar.</p>
<p><strong>Additional Notes:</strong> All critiques helpful! Something I would like to ask is how would I put this into an article? How do I describe something that cannot be described?</p>

