<p><strong>My thoughts</strong> - <em>Note: I am not an experienced reviewer, and cannot give greenlights.</em></p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;Show</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-&nbsp;Hide</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>I hope it hasn't been done before.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Unfortunately, this idea has been done before - notably in <a href="/scp-055">SCP-055</a>, but also in <a href="/scp-579">SCP-579</a> and <a href="/scp-5790">SCP-5790</a>. The idea of an anomaly with information being anomalously hard to gain is already called 'antimemetics' - a pretty well-explored concept on the wiki - but that doesn't mean a concept like this wouldn't work. For starters, expand your concept. It's hard to review a story without elaboration on the storyline, as even a bland anomaly can have an interesting storyline. Secondly, see what works in other antimemetic articles. In the articles I gave, its the information they do have, not the information they don't, that makes them interesting - the use of bizarre containment procedures hint at a threat that cant be talked about, and so on. I wouldn't recommend doing these formulas exactly, but try and do something interesting with the concept of a self-redacting anomaly. Finally; if you are gonna go down a comedic route, I just don't think 'so what is it?' will work very well, as the idea has already been used seriously a ton, so it doesn't mesh well, for experienced readers - but that's just my opinion. If you revise this idea, feel free to PM me, and I'll look over it again.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>Hope that helps!</p>

