<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">&gt;&nbsp;Further&nbsp;thoughts&nbsp;&lt;&nbsp;</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">hide</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>The idea now is more developed, which is great to see. The idea that the staff know what the anomaly is but you don't could go in many directions with it. Some questions:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>You cannot speak of it, cannot read it, cannot hear of it, and cannot see it.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>You say later on that the staff know what the anomaly is, but can't convey it to you. How do they figure out if you can't perceive it? It could be interesting to see the ingenuity of the foundation staff in this case. Perhaps you could convey the actual anomaly subtly through the article (al la <a href="/scp-4428">SCP-4428</a>)</p>
<blockquote>
<p>This SCP came into existence when a scientist attempted to document an anomaly he had discovered, only to realize he couldn't.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Did the antimemetic effects come from the interaction between the anomaly and the researcher? Or did were the effects already there? The wording here confuses me.</p>
<p>I would recommend that you tell reviewers what the actual anomaly is under the surface because I think an article would be more interesting if you could figure out what the anomaly is. Obviously, it doesn't have to be obvious, but antimemetic SCP's are often criticised for having no resolution, so I'd love to see what the anomaly is meant to be. Feel free to PM me if you revise the idea.</p>
</div>
